  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSurface high pressure and dry antecedent airmass remain firmly in \ncontrol across the Southern Appalachians and Tennessee Valley region \nfor the start of the period. Tonight and Friday's low temperatures \nhave been nudged lower, into the upper 20s to mid 30s to better \nmatch the recent trends with Efficient radiational cooling under \nmostly clear skies and calm winds. Current obs highlight \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 30s in some locations as of 11pm \nEST. The one limiting factor will be some thin high clouds currently \nover the NW CWA, but those should exit in the next few hours. \n\nFriday and Saturday will feature similar weather as the past few \ndays. Efficient afternoon mixing in the boundary layer will see \ntemperatures warm into the upper 60s and lower 70s as RH values \nbottom out in the upper teens to mid 20 percent range. We've opted \nto use the NBM 10th percentile for dewpoints given they line up much \ncloser to observations. Our primary concern will be the potential of \nwildfires given the combination of dry antecedent conditions and low \nRH values. A few have seemingly popped up across the TN Valley over \nthe last 48 hours and will need to be monitored. The one \"positive\" \nof the forecast will be that the light winds will limit how quickly \nany fires can grow. \n\nSaturday into Sunday, an area of low pressure off the Carolina coast \nis forecast to track inland across NC and VA. There is uncertainty \nwith how far west this sfc low will track. Current trends have \nfavored a slightly further western path as seen in recent ECMWF AIFS \nsolutions which could lead to some light rain in our far eastern \nareas. The more relevant concern will be the wind field associated \nwith this system. If the further west solution plays out it will \nlikely lead to an increased chance for strong downslope winds in the \neastern TN and southwestern VA mountains this weekend that would \nsignificantly increase the fire weather concerns. The current most \nlikely evolution keeps us just outside of that window, but close \nenough to monitor given the favorable fire weather conditions in \nplace ahead of this potential wind event.\n\nSunday appears mostly dry and mild with very modest moisture \nrecovery ahead of an approaching cold front. The front will move \nthrough Sunday night into Monday, bringing slight chc to chc PoPs \ninto the forecast. Guidance doesn't favor much sensible precipitation \nwith this fropa with most models making this a dry fropa. We start \nthe holiday week with cooler temperatures trending closer to \nseasonal normals. \n\nPrecipitation chances return Tuesday night into Wednesday as a \nnorthern stream trough digs southeast and begins to interact with a \nsouthern stream trough over the northern Gulf. As it's in the day 6 \nto 7 window... confidence in the exact evolution is low, but there \nis enough confidence in the signal for a system to impact the SE \nCONUS next Wednesday. This system will bring the potential for much \nneeded rain for the region at the expense of impacting \nThanksgiving Travel. For those with holiday travel plans \n- it'll likely be worth monitoring over the next several days!",
